at the minimum level a value chain  consists of a supplier consists of  component suppliers of assemblies final  assembly manufacturing distributors  retailers wholesalers and ultimately  could be the end consumers and its whole  chain of network that are engaged in the  production of products and services  that's the basic value chain but now  increasingly value chain could include  the government because they could affect  and they could limit they could restrict  or they could support your your whole  delivery of products and services it  could include even NGOs because they are  also a stakeholder they could also  include logistics providers third-party  providers so I call that the complete  value chain or the ecosystem  so today very few companies are  completely vertically integrated meaning  that you make everything you make all  the components yourself you make all the  sub assemblies yourself and you  distribute yourself everyone depend on  somebody else  so you depend on your suppliers to  depend on your outlet you depend on your  distributor you depend on some third  party as a channel and if you're not  able to integrate that value chain well  synchronize then you could be very  efficient you could be very successful  but if your value chain partners cannot  fulfill their job your products may not  be successful or your services cannot  reach to the customers in your intended  fashion and expectation so we're all  interdependent and in order for a  company to be successful the whole value  chain has to be successful  most of the entrepreneurs in the  developing economies recognized that  they have huge constraints because the  government may not be supportive if  their roles are bad the infrastructures  are not dependable their suppliers are  not on time so they all recognize these  challenges well so what I would tell  these entrepreneurs is that you can  overcome this these barriers are not  insurmountable and you overcome it by  sometimes redefining what your roles are  creating the right information linkages  creating the right relationships sharing  sharing collaborating and those  challenges and constraints can be  overcoming once they overcome there  could be new business models and new  products and services  then you have not even thought of today  and so that's why I my simple advice is  challenges are great but they can be  overcome by the right innovations and  approaches innovations that could  require you to rethink the way you  deliver your products we think about the  incentives that you give the suppliers  so sometimes those barriers are because  the suppliers did not trust you or  because the suppliers do not have the  right information or because the  suppliers have their own constraints and  so helping them by collaborating with  them you overcome their barriers which  in turn unblock your own bottleneck so I  would say that by going out having the  right information share having the right  collaborations in place having the right  incentives in place can go a long way  when we went to Ghana when we went to  these developing economies oftentimes  the entrepreneurs because of the  experience and because of the  limitations that they have lived with  all their lives they only think of Spa  markets they only think of okay what can  I do to create this service so that I  can serve my village so that I can serve  my town my own region my own geography  but in fact if they understand the  complete value chain well they can  export they can serve other countries  they can expand what they're doing I'll  give you an example of the farmers that  I work with in Ghana  these are SIA butter garnish farmers the  women farmers so they roll share butter  which are nuts but it turns out that the  biggest value add is if they can also  learn how to process the nuts so that  they become ingredients for cosmetic  companies and that is a much higher  value add than just purely growing the  nuts using the nuts for food consumption  their own people consuming the nuts and  so this is really about a way of  understanding what role you play in the  big value chain and can you add more  value by doing more than what you're  doing today and by expanding the  geographic span that you can cover  such innovations and not just for the  sake of helping the developed economies  so that they would create values for  themselves and make people live better  there I would have to say that if you  are a multinational today large  corporations we also our own students  MPA students our own alumni we also  better understand those economies better  we also better learn how to leverage the  entrepreneurial power of those economies  matter we also need to leverage the  innovations that could come from  so-called bottom of the pyramid because  those innovations can also be  incorporated into these if even if  you're a big corporation because make  corporations often time have to expand  and you may one day go to those  economies those economies could be your  market this is like about 30 years ago  25 years ago people would think of China  as a developing economy and you would  only use China as a place where you can  have cheap labor as a source to build  your products to manufacture your  products but today China is no longer a  developing economy even though pockets  of China are still developing but China  is a market China is a big market China  is a source of innovation same thing  could happen in different parts of  Africa in different parts of South Asia  in different parts of South America so I  would say that while we go out there  with the intent to help the  entrepreneurs in the region but I think  regardless of whether you are looking at  the interest of big corporations in  developed economies this kind of value  chain innovations are crucial for the  overall economic development of everyone
 

